# API design

Public contracts are Type 1. Do not expose persistence rows as the API. Version breaking changes deliberately.

Use consistent resource names, pagination, validation errors, and status codes. Idempotent methods stay idempotent; unsafe retries use idempotency keys where duplicates hurt.

Authentication identifies the caller. Authorization decides the tenant and resource. Document both boundaries.

Error bodies are stable and actionable. Do not leak stack traces, SQL, or secret names.

Write examples that match the schema. If an SDK or CLI is in scope, design the HTTP shape so a thin client can wrap it without a second model.
